(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-2-hearing-taste-smell-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"The Sense of Hearing, Taste, and Smell — Physiology Exam 2 — Set 2","category":"Physiology","questions":[{"q":"Besides the tongue, where else are taste buds found?","choices":["Only on the hard palate and the inner surface of the cheeks","Only within the nasal cavity and along the eustachian tube","Nowhere else — taste buds exist only on the tongue and lips","On the tonsillar pillars, palate, epiglottis, and proximal esophagus"],"answer":3,"exp":"Extraglossal taste buds are found on the tonsillar pillars, palate, epiglottis, and proximal esophagus, in addition to the three papillae types on the tongue itself. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How much of taste adaptation is accounted for by adaptation of the taste buds themselves?","choices":["About 90%, with only a small remainder attributed to a poorly understood central mechanism","About 100%, since central taste pathways play essentially no role in adaptation at all","Taste adapts fully within about 2 seconds, entirely at the level of the taste bud itself","Only about 50%, with the remainder attributed to central adaptation via an unknown mechanism"],"answer":3,"exp":"Taste sensations adapt rapidly, but adaptation of the taste buds themselves accounts for only about 50% of total adaptation; central adaptation must also occur, though its mechanism is not well understood. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Where is the olfactory membrane located?","choices":["In the middle ear","On the tongue only","On the inferior part of each nostril","On the superior part of each nostril"],"answer":3,"exp":"The olfactory membrane, which contains olfactory cells with cilia bearing odorant-binding protein receptors, is located on the superior part of each nostril. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What second-messenger pathway underlies olfactory signal transduction?","choices":["Odorants are transduced via changes in membrane lipid composition, activating phospholipase C to release IP3 directly","Direct opening of ligand-gated calcium channels, bypassing G-proteins and any cyclic nucleotide second messenger","Binding of odorant to receptor activates a G-protein, which activates adenylyl cyclase, producing cAMP that opens sodium channels causing depolarization","Odorant binding activates a G-protein that inhibits adenylyl cyclase, lowering cAMP and causing hyperpolarization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Binding of a chemical odorant to its receptor on olfactory cilia induces G-protein-mediated activation of adenylyl cyclase, producing cAMP, which opens sodium channels and causes depolarization — the beginning of olfactory signal transduction. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Approximately how many genes code for olfactory receptor neurons (ORNs), and how many actually encode working receptors?","choices":["~1000 genes total; only about 400 encode working ORNs","About 100 genes total; roughly half encode working ORNs","~500 genes total; nearly all 500 encode working ORNs","Exactly 50 genes code for all functioning ORNs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Approximately 1000 different genes code for olfactory receptor neurons, but only about 400 of these genes encode working ORNs; each ORN can bind a variety of odorants with varying affinities, producing unique activation patterns for different odorant profiles. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What three physical/chemical properties must a substance have to be smelled?","choices":["It must be solid and heavy at room temperature, entirely water-insoluble, and completely lipid-insoluble to be reliably perceived by receptors","It must be volatile (to be sniffed into the nostrils), at least slightly water soluble (to penetrate mucus), and at least slightly lipid soluble (to interact with the membrane)","It must be non-volatile and dense, entirely lipid-insoluble, and capable of dissolving completely within the surrounding nasal mucus layer","It must be entirely water-insoluble, completely non-volatile, and able to pass directly through the bony cribriform plate structure itself"],"answer":1,"exp":"To be smelled, a substance must be volatile (so it can be sniffed into the nostrils), at least slightly water soluble (to penetrate the mucus and reach olfactory cells), and at least slightly lipid soluble (to interact with the cell membrane). — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How does the adaptation rate of olfactory receptors compare to the adaptation rate of the overall smell sensation?","choices":["Both the receptors and the overall sensation adapt at essentially the same moderately rapid rate within seconds","Olfactory receptors adapt very slowly, but the overall olfactory sensation adapts rather rapidly, implying a central adaptation mechanism","Neither the receptors nor the overall sensation ever adapt, unlike virtually every other sensory modality in the body","Olfactory receptors adapt rapidly within milliseconds, while the overall sensation adapts only after several minutes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Olfactory receptors themselves adapt very slowly, but the perceived olfactory sensation adapts rather rapidly — this mismatch implies that a central (brain-level) adaptation mechanism must be involved, similar to the situation with taste. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Are the commonly cited \"primary sensations of smell\" (camphoraceous, musky, floral, pepperminty, ethereal, pungent, putrid) considered scientifically definitive?","choices":["No — they are based on psychological testing, and it is certain this list does not represent the true primary sensations of smell","Yes — genetic sequencing of all olfactory receptor types has confirmed these seven as the definitive primary smells","There is only one primary sensation of smell, universally agreed upon across all psychological studies","They were derived directly from sequencing the roughly 400 functional olfactory receptor genes"],"answer":0,"exp":"The commonly cited primary sensations of smell (camphoraceous, musky, floral, pepperminty, ethereal, pungent, putrid) are based on psychological testing; it is certain this list does not represent the true primary sensations of smell — unlike, say, the well-defined primary taste qualities. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which cranial nerve is dedicated to the sense of smell?","choices":["CN IX (Glossopharyngeal)","CN II (Optic)","CN I (Olfactory)","CN VIII (Vestibulocochlear)"],"answer":2,"exp":"The olfactory nerve is the first cranial nerve (CN I), transmitting smell sensation to the CNS via medial (old) and lateral (new) olfactory areas. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How well-developed and understood is the sense of smell in humans, according to this lecture?","choices":["It is the best understood of all the senses and is more highly developed in humans than in other mammals","It is fully understood mechanistically but functions no differently than taste at the receptor level","Smell and taste are entirely separate senses, with smell being far better characterized scientifically","It is the least understood of all the senses and is poorly developed in humans (compared to many other animals)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Smell is described as the least understood of all the senses, and it is poorly developed in humans compared to many other animals. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What primitive/survival functions do taste and smell serve, beyond flavor perception?","choices":["They have essentially no survival relevance in modern humans, having been almost entirely replaced functionally by visual and auditory food assessment","They only matter for social communication between individuals, playing no role in food selection or detecting spoiled or toxic substances","They allow separation of undesirable/lethal foods from nutritious ones and help recognize the proximity of other individuals or animals, tied to primitive emotional/behavioral functions","They function solely for the aesthetic enjoyment of food, with no meaningful connection to primitive emotional or protective survival behaviors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Taste and smell allow an organism to separate undesirable or lethal foods from nutritious ones and to recognize the proximity of other individuals or animals — functions tied to primitive emotional and behavioral functions of the nervous system. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What causes salty taste perception, and what is the approximate threshold concentration for NaCl?","choices":["H+ ions from acids, with a threshold of about 2 mM for citric acid","Ionized salts (mainly sodium), with a threshold of about 10 mM for NaCl","Glutamate compounds, with a threshold of about 0.03 mM for monosodium glutamate","Long-chain nitrogen alkaloids, with a threshold near 0.0001 mM"],"answer":1,"exp":"Salty taste is caused by ionized salts, mainly sodium, with an approximate threshold of 10 mM for NaCl. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What class of chemicals typically produces sweet taste, and what is the approximate threshold for sucrose?","choices":["Only ionized sodium salts, with a threshold near 10 mM for the primary compound","Only H+ ions from acids, with a threshold of about 2 mM for citric acid","Many chemicals, mostly organic compounds, with a threshold for sucrose of about 20 mM","Long-chain nitrogen alkaloids, with a threshold as low as 0.0001 mM for strychnine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sweet taste is produced by many chemicals, mostly organic compounds, with an approximate threshold for sucrose of about 20 mM — a much higher (less sensitive) threshold compared to bitter compounds. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"How does the threshold for detecting bitter compounds (e.g., quinine, strychnine) compare to the threshold for detecting sweet or salty compounds?","choices":["Bitter compounds require far higher concentrations to detect than salty compounds — e.g., quinine at 20 mM versus NaCl at 10 mM","All taste thresholds fall within a fairly narrow range near 10 mM, regardless of which specific compound is tested","Bitter thresholds are far lower (much more sensitive) than sweet or salty thresholds — e.g., strychnine detected at 0.0001 mM versus sucrose at 20 mM","Bitter thresholds are moderately higher than sour thresholds — e.g., quinine at 8 mM versus citric acid at 2 mM"],"answer":2,"exp":"Bitter compounds have far lower detection thresholds (much higher sensitivity) than sweet or salty compounds — for example, strychnine can be detected at 0.0001 mM and quinine at 0.008 mM, compared to sucrose's 20 mM threshold — consistent with bitter taste's role as a poison-detection survival mechanism. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Where are foliate papillae located, and what fraction of taste buds do they contain?","choices":["Along the lateral surfaces of the tongue, containing about 25% of taste buds","Along the anterior tip of the tongue, containing about 10% of taste buds","Deep within the tongue's musculature, containing no functional taste buds","On the soft palate near the uvula, containing about 5% of taste buds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Foliate papillae are located along the lateral surfaces of the tongue and contain about 25% of taste buds, the same proportion found on fungiform papillae (located over the flat surface of the tongue); circumvallate papillae, forming a V on the posterior tongue, contain the remaining 50%. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the correct order of relay stations for taste signals traveling from the tongue to conscious perception?","choices":["Taste fibers → cortex → solitary nucleus → thalamus","Taste fibers → thalamus → solitary nucleus → cortex","Taste fibers → solitary nucleus → thalamus → cortex","Taste fibers → cortex directly, bypassing all relay nuclei"],"answer":2,"exp":"Taste fibers (from CN VII, IX, and X depending on tongue region) all transmit signals to the solitary nucleus first, which relays to the thalamus, which relays to the cortex for conscious perception. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"How do olfactory receptor neurons (ORNs) generate the perception of many distinct odors despite having a limited number of receptor gene types?","choices":["A given ORN can bind a variety of odorants with varying affinities; differences in these affinities create differing activation patterns across ORNs, producing unique odorant profiles","Odor perception depends entirely on the concentration of odorant reaching the nasal mucosa, regardless of which receptors are activated","All odorants produce an essentially identical activation pattern across every one of the roughly 400 functional receptor types simultaneously","Each odorant activates exactly one dedicated receptor type with no overlap, requiring 10,000 distinct genes for full odor discrimination"],"answer":0,"exp":"ORNs can bind to a variety of odorants with varying affinities; these differences in binding affinity create differing patterns of ORN activation across the roughly 400 working receptor types, producing the unique activation \"profile\" that the brain interprets as a specific odor. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What are the two olfactory areas that receive input from the olfactory nerve, and how are they characterized?","choices":["Superior and inferior olfactory areas, arranged with no evolutionary significance","Only one undivided olfactory area exists, located entirely within the temporal lobe","Dorsal and ventral olfactory areas, both equally recent in evolutionary terms","A medial (older, evolutionarily) olfactory area and a lateral (newer) olfactory area"],"answer":3,"exp":"The olfactory nerve (CN I) projects to a medial (\"old\") olfactory area and a lateral (\"new\") olfactory area, reflecting the evolutionary layering of olfactory processing regions in the brain. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"What happens to the ability to discriminate sound patterns when the auditory cortex, with its tonotopic map organization, is destroyed?","choices":["Only high-frequency discrimination is lost, low-frequency is unaffected","Sound pattern discrimination is unaffected","Hearing sensitivity actually improves","Discrimination of sound patterns is lost"],"answer":3,"exp":"The auditory cortex and its association areas are organized by tonotopic maps (high frequency at one end, low at the other); when the auditory cortex is destroyed, discrimination of sound patterns is lost even if basic sound detection may persist at lower levels. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"How do basilar membrane fiber length and diameter change from the base of the cochlea to the helicotrema?","choices":["Fiber length increases and diameter decreases toward the helicotrema","Fiber length and diameter both increase toward the helicotrema","Fiber length decreases and diameter increases toward the helicotrema","Both length and diameter stay constant"],"answer":0,"exp":"From the base (near the oval window) to the helicotrema, basilar membrane fiber length increases while fiber diameter decreases, producing the roughly 100-fold decrease in stiffness that underlies the place principle of frequency detection. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Approximately how many times more outer hair cells are there compared to inner hair cells in the organ of Corti?","choices":["There are more inner hair cells than outer hair cells","3-4 times as many outer hair cells","10 times as many outer hair cells","Equal numbers of each"],"answer":1,"exp":"There are approximately 3-4 times as many outer hair cells as inner hair cells, even though inner hair cells transmit about 90% of auditory signals — outer hair cells are thought to modulate inner hair cell sensitivity. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"How many of the 13 possible taste bud chemoreceptor types are dedicated to detecting sodium?","choices":["0","All 13","2","5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Of the 13 possible chemical receptor types found in taste buds, 2 are dedicated to sodium detection (alongside 2 for potassium, plus receptors for chloride, adenosine, inosine, 2 sweet, 2 bitter, glutamate, and hydrogen ion). — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which bitter compound has a lower detection threshold — quinine or strychnine?","choices":["Strychnine, at 0.0001 mM, is far more sensitive (detectable at lower concentration) than quinine at 0.008 mM","Both have thresholds of exactly 0.001 mM, making them equally detectable at low concentrations","Neither compound activates bitter receptors; both instead stimulate only sour taste pathways","Quinine, detectable at 0.00001 mM, is far more sensitive than strychnine at 0.008 mM"],"answer":0,"exp":"Strychnine has a threshold of about 0.0001 mM, making it detectable at a much lower concentration (i.e., more sensitively perceived) than quinine, which has a threshold of about 0.008 mM. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the approximate detection threshold for citric acid, producing the sour taste sensation?","choices":["2 mM","200 mM","0.008 mM","20 mM"],"answer":0,"exp":"The threshold for detecting citric acid (which produces sour taste via H+ ions) is approximately 2 mM. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Approximately how many taste buds does the human tongue contain?","choices":["100,000-500,000","3,000-10,000","300-1,000","Over 1 million"],"answer":1,"exp":"The human tongue contains approximately 3,000 to 10,000 taste buds, with an average individual taste bud lifespan of about 10 days. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Where exactly are odorant-binding protein receptors located within the olfactory system?","choices":["On the cilia of olfactory cells","On the tectorial membrane","Only in the olfactory bulb neurons","On the tongue's fungiform papillae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Odorant-binding protein receptors are located on the cilia of olfactory cells within the olfactory membrane; binding of a chemical odorant to these receptors triggers the G-protein/cAMP transduction cascade. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Approximately what frequency is a typical human voice, which the attenuation reflex helps dampen?","choices":["~100 Hz","~10,000 Hz","~100,000 Hz","~1000 Hz"],"answer":3,"exp":"The attenuation reflex helps dampen low-frequency sounds such as one's own voice, approximately 1000 Hz, protecting the cochlea and reducing the perceived loudness of self-generated or others' speech. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Why is it functionally important that the tympanic membrane's area is much larger than the oval window's area?","choices":["It only matters for very loud sounds above 100 decibels, providing essentially no benefit during ordinary conversational-level hearing","The large area-to-small-area ratio concentrates the force of airborne sound vibrations, amplifying the pressure delivered to the fluid-filled cochlea at the oval window","It has essentially no functional importance, since the round window alone provides all necessary impedance matching for the cochlea","It slows the transmission of sound by several milliseconds, protecting the delicate cochlea from sudden transient pressure spikes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Because the tympanic membrane's area is about 17 times larger than the oval window's, the force collected over the larger membrane area is concentrated onto the much smaller oval window, amplifying pressure and helping overcome the impedance mismatch between air and cochlear fluid. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Do all five basic taste qualities (salty, sweet, sour, bitter, umami) use the identical transduction mechanism to depolarize taste cells?","choices":["Only sour taste causes depolarization via direct H+ channel blockade; the other four taste qualities rely on non-electrical transduction mechanisms","No taste quality causes true membrane depolarization; all five instead signal exclusively through direct synaptic vesicle release","Yes, all five taste qualities use an identical G-protein-coupled receptor pathway that strictly requires external calcium entry to function","No — salty, sweet, sour, and umami cause depolarization via different mechanisms, while bitter uniquely relies on internal Ca2+ release with no external calcium required"],"answer":3,"exp":"Salty, sweet, sour, and umami tastes each cause depolarization of their respective taste cells via distinct mechanisms, while bitter taste transduction is unique in causing an internal release of Ca2+ without requiring external calcium entry. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What structure encases and protects the cochlea?","choices":["The tympanic membrane alone","Bone","Only skin","Cartilage"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cochlea is encased in bone, providing rigid protection for its delicate fluid-filled structures. — 7. Hearing, Tatse, and Smell.pptx, Slide 6"}]};
